>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">HOA & Exterior Paint</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">HOA & Exterior Paint in Osceola County</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        HOA exterior paint rules in Kissimmee, Celebration, St. Cloud, and Poinciana. Approval process, color restrictions, and avoiding fines.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Most <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a>, and <a href="/areas/poinciana" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Poinciana</a> neighborhoods have HOAs (homeowners associations) that regulate exterior paint colors. You can't just pick a color and start <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">painting</a>—you need HOA approval first, or you risk fines and forced repaints. Here's how the process works and what to watch for.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why HOAs Control Exterior Paint</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            HOAs exist to maintain property values and neighborhood aesthetics. Allowing homeowners to paint houses neon orange or lime green would hurt resale values and curb appeal. Most Central Florida HOAs restrict exterior colors to neutrals—beiges, tans, grays, whites—with occasional allowances for muted blues, greens, or earth tones.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Celebration's HOA is famously strict (Disney built the town with tight design controls). Older Kissimmee and Poinciana communities vary—some are relaxed, some enforce aggressively. Always check your specific HOA rules before painting.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Approval Process</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 1: Review HOA Color Guidelines</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Your HOA should have a list of pre-approved colors or a palette range. Some HOAs publish the list on their website or include it in the CC&Rs (covenants, conditions, and restrictions) you received when you bought the house. If you can't find it, call or email the HOA management company.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 2: Submit an Architectural Review Request</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most HOAs require a formal request before you paint. This usually includes:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Paint color samples (names and brand codes)</li>
            <li>Photos of your house</li>
            <li>Description of what you're painting (whole house, trim only, front door, etc.)</li>
            <li>Contractor name and contact info (if using one)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Submit this to the HOA board or architectural review committee (ARC). Turnaround time varies—1 to 4 weeks is typical. Don't start painting before approval.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 3: Wait for Approval (or Denial)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            The HOA reviews your request at their next board meeting. They approve, deny, or ask for modifications. Common denial reasons:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Color not on approved list</li>
            <li>Too bright or too dark (subjective, but HOAs enforce it)</li>
            <li>Doesn't match neighborhood aesthetic</li>
            <li>Incomplete application (missing samples or photos)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If denied, you can appeal, submit a different color, or argue your case at the next meeting. Some HOAs are negotiable, some aren't.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 4: Paint & Submit Completion Notice</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Once approved, paint the house. Some HOAs require a completion notice or follow-up inspection to confirm you used the approved color. If you skip this, they may assume you violated the rules and send a violation letter.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Happens If You Skip Approval?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you paint without HOA approval, expect:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Violation letter:</strong> HOA sends a notice that you violated the rules</li>
            <li><strong>Fines:</strong> $50-$200 per day until you fix it (Florida law caps HOA fines, but they add up)</li>
            <li><strong>Forced repaint:</strong> HOA may require you to repaint at your own expense in an approved color</li>
            <li><strong>Lien on property:</strong> In extreme cases, unpaid fines become liens that block refinancing or selling</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Key point:</strong> Even if you think the HOA's color restrictions are ridiculous, you agreed to them when you bought the house. Courts almost always side with HOAs on paint violations. Get approval first—it's not optional.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common HOA Paint Rules in Central Florida</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Body Color</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most Osceola County HOAs allow neutrals: white, beige, tan, gray, cream. Some permit muted pastels (light blue, sage green, soft yellow). Bright or dark colors (navy, forest green, burgundy) are usually rejected unless the neighborhood has existing precedent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Trim Color</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <a href="/services/trim-crown-molding" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Trim</a> (window frames, door frames, fascia, soffits) is usually required to be white or a lighter shade than the body color. Some HOAs allow contrasting trim (dark body, light trim), others require minimal contrast.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Front Door</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Front doors often get more flexibility—bold colors (red, blue, black) may be allowed even if body colors are restricted. But not always. Check your HOA rules specifically for doors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Shutters & Accents</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Shutters, garage doors, and decorative accents follow the same approval process. Changing shutter color from white to black? Submit a request.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Strictest vs. Most Relaxed HOAs</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Strictest</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Celebration:</strong> Disney-built town with aggressive design controls. Very limited color palette, lengthy approval process, high enforcement. Don't even think about painting without approval.<br />
            <strong>Newer gated communities:</strong> Windsor at Westside, Solterra Resort, Storey Lake. Strict rules to maintain resort aesthetics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">More Relaxed</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Older Kissimmee neighborhoods (built pre-2000):</strong> Many have HOAs but enforcement is inconsistent. Rules exist but violations may not be pursued aggressively.<br />
            <strong>Poinciana:</strong> Large community with varying enforcement. Some sections strict, some lax. Check your specific sub-HOA.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Tips for Getting Approval Faster</h2>
          
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Pick from the pre-approved list.</strong> If your HOA publishes approved colors, choose one—approval is automatic or near-instant.</li>
            <li><strong>Submit physical samples.</strong> Photos of paint chips don't always convey color accurately. Some HOAs want actual painted boards to review.</li>
            <li><strong>Look at neighbors.</strong> If three houses on your street have gray exteriors, gray is likely approved. Match existing precedent.</li>
            <li><strong>Be patient.</strong> HOA boards meet monthly (sometimes less). Plan ahead—submit 4-6 weeks before you need to paint.</li>
            <li><strong>Hire a contractor familiar with your HOA.</strong> We paint in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, and <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a> HOA communities regularly and know which colors typically pass.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What If the HOA Is Unreasonable?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you think your HOA's denial is arbitrary or inconsistent with other homes, you can:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Appeal the decision.</strong> Request a hearing at the next board meeting and present your case.</li>
            <li><strong>Point out precedent.</strong> If neighbors have similar colors without violations, argue inconsistent enforcement.</li>
            <li><strong>Negotiate a compromise.</strong> Offer a slightly different shade or limit the change to trim/doors.</li>
            <li><strong>Consult an HOA attorney (last resort).</strong> If the HOA is truly violating Florida law or their own bylaws, a lawyer can help—but legal fights are expensive.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            HOA exterior paint approval in Osceola County is mandatory for most communities. Submit a formal request with color samples before painting, wait for approval (1-4 weeks), then proceed. Skipping approval risks fines, forced repaints, and legal headaches. Pick neutral colors from pre-approved lists for fastest approval. Strict HOAs (Celebration, newer gated communities) enforce aggressively; older neighborhoods vary.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Help with HOA-Approved Exterior Painting?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
